Level 11 → Level 12

Concept
This level uses ROT13, a simple substitution cipher where every letter 
gets shifted 13 places through the alphabet (so A becomes N, B becomes 
O, and so on - and because there are 26 letters, shifting by 13 twice 
gets you back to the original). It's not real encryption, just letter 
substitution, but if you've never seen it before it looks completely 
unreadable at first glance.

Commands used
tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m' < data.txt

Why it works
tr translates one set of characters into another, character by 
character. The first range (A-Za-z) is "every letter, upper and 
lowercase." The second range (N-ZA-Mn-za-m) is that same alphabet 
rotated 13 positions, so each letter gets swapped for the one 13 
places ahead. Feed the scrambled file in through < and tr rewrites 
every letter on the way past, which flips the ROT13 text back into 
plain readable English - password included.
